WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.170
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.781
It may be the New Year, but we’ve still
got a massive hangover from 2017’s final

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:19.520
blockbuster Bright, a film that left us cross-eyed
and confused.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.480
Netflix spent 90 million dollars combining
two of our favorite things- the dystopian

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.690
fantasy and the buddy cop -- and left us with
the world’s most expensive “WTF?”

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:33.750
We’ve been scratching our heads trying to
figure how such an awesome concept -- think

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.340
Rush Hour 2 of the Deathly Hallows or Lethal
Weapon of the Rings ended up feeling as big

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.140
as shitshow as David Ayer’s last movie.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.160
Like other fantasy films, it SEEMS to be using
its imaginary setting to tell a compelling

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.180
story, while also illuminating truths about
our society.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:55.989
However, both its plot AND any potential underlying
meaning become completely muddled by the film’s

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:01.149
poor world-building, lousy character development,
and ill-suited metaphors about American racial

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:02.149
politics.

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:07.460
So let’s find out what happened in this
week’s Wisecrack Edition on Bright- “What

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.610
Went Wrong.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.179
As always, major, minor and vaguely judgemental
spoilers ahead.

00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:20.060
Head Scratcher #1: Building the World.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.920
Every time you see a film, you’re asked
to take a leap of faith and accept some kind

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.200
of proposition - that loving parents occasionally
leave their youngest kid behind when they

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.700
go on vacation or that Keanu Reeves is the
most badass assassin EVER.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.899
But when you’re watching a fantasy that
plays with gravity, object permanence or the

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:42.070
entertainment potential of a wardrobe filmmakers
have to convince you to take a Mount Everest-sized

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.819
leap off a peak of disbelief and land with
their characters in a distant galaxy, Middle

00:01:46.819 --> 00:01:49.780
Earth, or a far away distant land in a wardrobe.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.130
A good script gets the audience to buy into
its world by explaining the rules of that

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.670
world coherently and concisely… and getting
the heck on with the story.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.010
Think of how effective Lord of the Rings is
in briefing the audience on the history of

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.050
Middle Earth’s most sought-after jewelry.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.319
“It began with the forging of the Great
Rings….

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:14.239
Three were given to the Elves...Seven to the
Dwarf Lords...And Nine ... were gifted to

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:16.290
the race of Men…

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:23.760
But they were all of them deceived...for another
Ring was made... a Master Ring to control

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.760
all others...

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.239
One Ring to rule them all!”

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:32.950
With a origin story clocking in at 6 minutes,
the film situates us firmly in this complex

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:33.950
world.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.519
For an even leaner example, consider Star
Wars epic rolling title card that introduces

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:41.879
you to an intergalactic space war in the time
it takes to unwrap a candy bar.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:45.890
Both films orient audiences quickly, and then
they’re off to the races.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.610
Bright’s origin story, on the other hand,
is just plain messy; divulged in sloppy, repetitive,

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.640
yet incomplete fragments.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.621
But before we analyze the world-building strategies,
a brief overview of the myth behind it: Two

00:02:58.621 --> 00:03:02.000
thousand years ago something gnarly went down
with a Dark Lord.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.579
All the Orcs sided with him, except this one
guy, Jirak, who somehow united the nine races

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:08.629
together (Who are the nine races??

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:14.079
Unclear - We’ve got elves, humans, orcs,
fairies, and, for the eagle-eyed viewer, centaurs)

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:16.720
But anyway, they managed to take down the
Dark Lord.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.969
Except oops, he’s ready to come back, and
all his demon elves need to do is bring three

00:03:20.969 --> 00:03:22.409
wands together.

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:26.359
Watch out though, only a Bright can hold a
wand, which creates complications.

00:03:26.359 --> 00:03:30.170
Still, even if the Dark Lord does rise, he
can be stopped with magic…

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.849
That whole subplot never comes to fruition
though - probably to be saved for the recently-greenlit

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:36.409
sequel that will hopefully come with some
clarification.

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:40.560
Now, it’s totally understandable that the
minds behind Bright wanted to veer away from

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.249
the dramatically-narrated story-book-style
opener of Lord of the Rings.

00:03:44.249 --> 00:03:47.260
It’s not exactly the freshest turf in the
screenwriting world.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:51.519
Or it... would be understandable, if Bright
hadn’t started out with an equally cheesy

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:55.189
but less eloquent quote that sounds like it
came out of a 10-year-old’s Dungeon and

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:56.239
Dragons game.

00:03:56.239 --> 00:04:00.180
From there, they have an opening sequence
of cringeworthy tagging (You’d think with

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.349
90 million dollars they could afford to hire
a decent LA graffiti artist??)

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:07.269
Through this, we learn that there’s inter-species
upheaval, police brutality, and…

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:09.550
Lizard People roaming the streets?

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:14.319
It pretty rude to tease the existence of Lizard
People and not deliver, but I digress.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:18.160
These shots of the street explain some atmospheric
aspects of the film’s world but we’re

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.160
still missing the cold hard facts.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.380
For instance, who is the Dark Lord?

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.280
And what did he do?

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.960
Why are all the social media influencers Elves?

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.530
We weren’t the only people scratching our
heads.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.169
After fans expressed widespread confusion
as to what the heck was going on with this

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:39.060
Dark Lord stuff, Netflix released what amounted
to a 2-minute INSTRUCTION MANUAL for the film.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.280
In it, we were given a few thousands years
of magical history, with factual nuggets like:

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.550
“Jirak was hailed as the first Bright.”

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:49.520
But if Jirak saved everyone, why do people
hate orcs so damn much??

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.040
And how did this world’s social order come
about?

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.400
This video feels more like a hastily-concocted
admission of guilt that this film was confusing

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.680
as hell.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.349
Another world-building issue is the way the
film conveys the limited information it does

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:02.460
tell the audience.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.510
Which brings us to a fundamental screenwriting
rule: “showing vs. telling.”

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:10.220
Showing is when a film conveys information
through characters’ actions.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.750
Telling is when characters chat about the
events of the world - the narrative equivalent

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.979
of an intel briefing for the audience.

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:18.910
And boy is Bright is a tell-a-thon.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:23.000
Throughout the film, characters regularly
serve as embedded narrators, a strategy that

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.460
swallows the action and creates stiff, unrealistic
dialogue.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.340
For instance, the award for worst line in
the film goes to Ward’s daughter in this

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:32.340
cringer.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:37.290
"Why do you have to be a policeman?

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:38.290
Everyone hates policemen."

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:39.290
OK.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:40.290
We get it.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:43.879
Another prime example comes from Ward early
on: “Hey look, I got a dude in my car, the

00:05:43.879 --> 00:05:45.550
whole world is watching, aight.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.000
I didn’t ask for it.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.000
I don’t want it.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.610
But I don’t get a vote.”

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.220
First of all, this speech is stupendously
heavy handed.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.789
Nobody talks like this.

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:57.050
Secondly, these facts can be SHOWN in a natural
way through the narrative.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:01.310
In fact, Ward makes it PRETTY clear he’d
rather be shat on by a Fairy then ride to

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.940
work alongside Jakoby.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:16.940
Still, the winner for most forced proxy narrator
has to be the Smelly Sword-Wielding Bum, who

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:21.949
goes from trying to samurai a crowd of people
to calmly relaying the entire world’s backstory

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:23.240
without even a shower-break.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:29.009
“The army of nice races fought shoulder
to shoulder to give you the world you neglect...

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:33.979
and now the dark one returns to reclaim orc
hearts.”

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:38.389
Then as the Feds interrogate him, he gives
a plodding explanation of the shield of light,

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:42.250
Inferni, wands, the dark lord, Leyla, Tikka,
and Bright.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:48.050
This scene is A. boring and B. doesn’t make
sense unless you assume federal agents in

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:53.510
charge of Magic Crime are as fucking confused
by the rules of their world as we are.

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.039
This strategy continues throughout the film.

00:06:56.039 --> 00:07:00.250
Once Jakoby and Ward find the magic wand and
call for backup, four skeezy cops show up

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.680
and give us another forced lecture on...

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.020
How magic works.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.539
"That's magic right there.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:07.720
That's whatever you want.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.280
You want a million dollars?

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.259
You want ten million?

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:18.509
You wanna be taller or shorter or make your
dick bigger?

00:07:18.509 --> 00:07:21.979
You wanna go back in time and marry the girl
who didn't blow you on prom night?

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:23.370
That's how you do it right there."

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:27.600
They continue to narrate the plot, as if the
audience would be absolutely flummoxed by

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.330
the concept of dirty cops.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:36.770
It’s cleary done for the audiences’ sake,
and its corny as hell.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:41.820
The script spoon feeds us huge plot points
and small details alikes -- Like when Ward,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.650
Jakoby and Tikka are fleeing the Mexican gang
and Ward yells: "Windows up!

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:46.650
Bulletproof!"

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:48.349
Is he patronizing Jakoby, or the audience?

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:49.630
Who even knows.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.760
Just for fun, they remind us: "Bulletproof,
Dickhead!"

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.470
Needless plot exposition, dickhead.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:59.960
Sometimes this tiresome exposition manages
to somehow be both forced AND incomplete.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:05.509
For instance, Jakoby gives a snooze-inducing
AND scattered explanation of his Orcan Idol

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:09.169
Jirak: “He was a farmer who changed the
world!"

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:10.169
Ok and?

00:08:10.169 --> 00:08:15.600
"They raised their blades to him, and he was
blooded in that very moment."

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.860
Ok, so Jirak was a farmer who was… wait
what was that?

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.130
"Blooded in that very moment."

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.719
Ok, seriously, what is the deal with this
blooding thing?

00:08:24.719 --> 00:08:25.860
"He's not blooded.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.900
He's not a part of any blooded clan.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.969
He's like a finger that got cut off the hand.

00:08:30.969 --> 00:08:32.120
He's dead to us"

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:36.890
Jakoby, who is not blooded, explains that
it’s a sign of honour in the Orc community:

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:41.980
“What you did tonight, that’s what it
takes for an orc to get blooded.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:43.540
An act of great bravery.”

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:47.900
So, are we supposed to think that all these
other orcs are brave except Jacobi, the one

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.410
who professionally puts his life in harm’s
way?

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:52.530
And what does blooding even MEAN?

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:56.140
Jakoby's sad dental situation as compared
to his brethren suggests it’s definitely

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.310
tooth related.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:01.590
We finally get a peek at this blooding ritual
at the end of the film after Jakoby saves

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:07.360
Ward and the same orcs who..were… only half
an hour ago KILLED him slit their palms because

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.770
clan loyalty means never having to say you’re
sorry.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.010
At least that’s showing, not telling.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.010
Gold star.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:16.980
If only we understood what the heck is going
on or how this crowd of orcs knew that Jakoby

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.170
had just committed an act of bravery.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.170
Is there some kind of orc bat signal?

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:24.670
Were they just following Jakoby all night,
waiting to shed blood which would magically

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.620
give Jakoby the perfect orc smile?

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:30.810
On the bright side, the next day, Jakoby appears
to be teething on his new fangs.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:36.350
Moving on to Head Scratcher #2 - Hero, Schmero
- Whether you’re writing about outer space,

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:40.010
downtown Baltimore, or a wizarding school
in an English castle, the first ingredient

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.190
of a good story is a hero.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:43.190
Or two.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.190
Or three.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:49.130
In Bright we have Ward and Jakoby, and neither
will undergo significant character development

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:50.810
over the course of the film.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:10:00.480
Ward begins the film as a snarky dickhead,
and ends the film as a snarky dickhead.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.340
"Think you a big dog now.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:04.060
You blooded huh?"

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.780
Jakoby begins as a toothless insecure guy
who can’t stand up for himself, "How are

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:08.780
your holes?"

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:13.020
"How the fuck do you make a shootout awkward."

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.230
"Sorry.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.440
Sorry."

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:20.220
and ends the film as a slightly toothed guy
who Ward still treats like a tattooed Forrest

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.220
Gump.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:22.950
"Jakoby, thank the nice officers!"

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:29.820
Beyond that, for a film centered on an unlikely
friendship, their relationship also seems

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:30.820
pretty stagnant.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:35.750
At best, it could be said that Ward learns
to “tolerate” Jakoby and not narc on him,

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:38.270
which is pretty bleak as far as buddy films
go.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.700
It’s not surprising that they never change
though because they never really had character

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:42.870
journeys to begin with.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:48.090
The number one rule in making a compelling
hero, according to Aaron Sorkin, is that "Somebody's

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.740
gotta want something, something's got to be
standing in their way of getting it.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.650
You do that and you'll have a scene."

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.620
Eventually, you’ll have a movie too.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:00.350
Wesley wants Princess Buttercup, Ferris Bueller
wants to cut class and party, and so on.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.600
In Bright, not so much.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.520
Ward doesn’t appear to actively want anything
except maybe to lose Jakoby as a partner and

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.300
keep his job without being a corrupt asshole.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:13.800
That said, he doesn’t pursue those goals
in any meaningful way.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:17.880
Jakoby does want to be blooded but, as explained
earlier, we don’t really understand what

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.590
the heck being blooded is or what it means
for him as a character.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:25.330
Does he have to submit to clan loyalty and
hang out with those Miami orcs?

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:26.730
They seemed fun!

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:30.930
While a “want” makes a hero initially
compelling, the hero’s choices are what

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.910
makes a character stay compelling.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.290
Apparently, Dustin Hoffman won’t even sign
onto a movie unless his character has no less

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.960
than 40 points of choice in the script.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:44.790
Rather than 40 points of choice, our main
guys face approximately 40 points of coincidences

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.110
that save their butts.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:49.672
Such a coincidence is called a Deus Ex Machina,
or a “person or thing (as in fiction or

00:11:49.672 --> 00:11:54.270
drama) that appears suddenly and provides
a contrived solution to an apparently insoluble

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:55.270
difficulty.”

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:57.450
Most notable among these is the Wand.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:01.050
In one instance, the wand solves the inconvenience
of Jakoby dying.

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:05.170
In another, Ward’s totally random status
as a Bright turns what would have been an

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:10.390
act of suicide into a semi-heroic, 100% coincidental
feat.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:15.260
Now for Head Scratcher #3 - When a metaphor
is a train wreck.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:19.960
Bright is littered with unsubtle metaphors
comparing “interspecies discrimination”

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.430
with real world racial discrimination.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:25.430
Even Chance the Rapper called the metaphor
“a little shallow.”

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:30.060
We more than a little agree, but before we
take a deep dive into the meanings at play,

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:35.000
we want to give the following disclaimer:
Yes, this is a fictional world in which nine

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.180
races coexist with varying levels of friction.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:42.310
MAYBE the filmmakers had no intention of it
being read as a precise metaphor for American

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.680
racial politics.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:49.870
Problem is, the movie practically BEGS you
to draw this comparison.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:54.750
First off, it’s a film about COPS set in
LA, a city that was home to one of the country’s

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:59.450
most violent race riots EVER, one which was
specifically ignited by police violence towards

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:00.870
black folks.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.870
Bright VERY deliberately draws comparisons
between Orcs and black Americans in ways that

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.790
are too obvious to ignore.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:12.690
From the graffiti to the urban streetwear,
visual adaptations of black culture abound.

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:17.200
They’re marginalized by other races in society
and face victimisation at the hands of the

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:18.200
police.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:29.420
In truly sophisticated symbolism, the film
suggests the cops would definitely kill 

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.120
the “hooded orc.”

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:36.390
Just for fun, one orc tells Jakoby “Your
buddy is so happy to say he has an Orc friend.”

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:40.130
not knowing that Ward VERY dramatically “doesn’t
do friends."

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:44.520
Oh jeez dude, save it for your Centaur therapist.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:48.990
That metaphor is referenced most disturbingly
when Jakoby is hung by a rope during a fight

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.110
scene with an elf.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:53.700
I don’t need to spell out why putting a
character that has been signified as black

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.890
in this situation is seriously messed up.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:01.180
Oh, and one VERY important distinction between
Orcs and black Americans?

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:05.640
Orcs apparently CAUSED their oppression, or
at least their great-great-great-grand-orc

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.380
daddies did when they decided to side with
the Dark Lord.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:13.010
“Orcs chose the wrong side a long time ago
and they've been paying for it ever since."

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:16.640
Needless to say, real racial prejudice does
not work that way.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:17.640
I.e.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.940
Black Americans have zero sins to pay for.

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:24.040
When you’re making a metaphor about race,
it’s important to be very, very precise

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:28.410
- because there’s a real danger that an
ill conceived metaphor can muddle an already

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:30.150
complicated conversation.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:34.270
To understand why, let’s examine the cognitive
function of metaphors.

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:38.450
In their influential book, “Metaphors We
Live by,” scholars George Lakoff and Mark

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:42.780
Johnson explain that metaphors are vital to
human being’s understanding of the world

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:43.780
around us.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:48.220
That’s because metaphors are a way of taking
a human’s understanding of a simple, often

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:53.140
physically tangible concept and transferring
that understanding onto another, more complicated

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:56.940
concept, often one hidden from our senses
or difficult to explain.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:01.590
Lakoff &amp; Johnson argue that when we make a
metaphor, say comparing “madness” to “love,”

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:04.050
we’re not just momentarily linking two ideas.

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:09.350
We’re also transferring an extensive array
of our cognitive associations about what constitutes

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.180
f “madness” onto “love.”

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:16.010
As a result, a human struggling to write a
Valentine card can explain why they have butterflies

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.010
in their belly.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:21.680
As a result, “Metaphors may create realities
for us, especially social realities leading

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:22.680
us to “view..

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.190
The metaphor as being true.”

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.450
So in this case: Lovers be cray.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.950
SO what does all this have to do fictional
orcs?

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:33.720
When a film like Bright unsubtly evokes a
metaphor that transfers the viewer’s understanding

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:38.970
of the inhuman orcs onto “black Americans”
they inherently invite the possibility that

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:43.500
viewers will transfer all their cognitive
associations about what constitutes orcs -- a

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:49.010
“race” of homogenous, inarticulate tattooed
gangsters on to that of the extremely complex,

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:52.030
and most importantly HUMAN group of black
Americans.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:56.450
It’s not like Bright will radically harm
race relations, but this practice does place

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:01.470
it somewhere in an ugly history of American
cinema comparing people of color to inhuman

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:02.470
“creatures.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:06.070
Before we go further into this, it’s important
to address the colorblind elephant in the

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:10.580
room: Bright depicts a world where a black
man of Ward’s status can exist seemingly

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:11.870
without facing bigotry.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:16.250
Or to quote Chance the Rapper, “...racism
is gone... cause we hate ork now.”

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:20.560
That’s messed up - More so even when you
couple it with Ward’s treatment of marginalised

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:24.550
creatures, all of which evoke treatment of
black Americans by police officers.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.570
Whether he’s saying, "Fairy Lives Don't
Matter Today."

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:31.470
or watching a bunch of Orcs get beat up then
asking Jakoby: “I need to know if you’re

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:36.630
a cop first or an orc first…” it just
feels...odd.

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:40.980
In a country where violence against black
men by police officers remains heartbreakingly

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:45.650
common, depicting a black cop this way just
feels way way off the mark.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:49.820
Ward’s character seems to be there to both
protect the film against charges of racism

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:54.130
(How can a movie be racist when it shows a
black man as equal and even getting to be

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:59.200
racist himself!) and to make a backwards argument
about how “far” black America has come,

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:03.620
through an individual representation of a
black man whose “made it” and “earned

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:04.620
respect.”

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:08.199
Now back to the specifics of the film’s
depiction of Orcs, which make it even more

00:17:08.199 --> 00:17:09.199
icky.

00:17:09.199 --> 00:17:14.329
While Jakoby is an honorable guy with a working
moral compass, his depiction is not particularly

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:15.329
flattering.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:19.569
For starters, and there’s no easy way to
say this: Jakoby is not the sharpest.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:23.500
This is most painfully obvious near the end
of the film, when Jakoby cannot stop spilling

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.559
incriminating evidence to two federal agents.

00:17:25.559 --> 00:17:30.760
"He turned around, sight unseen, all four
of them, bang bang bang bang."

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:35.330
The film doesn’t make it better when Ward
tells his daughter: “All of the races are

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:36.330
... uh ... different."

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:40.470
While there’s something to be said for respecting
ethnic and cultural differences, Ward's comments

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:44.509
seem to suggest the existence of actual biological
racial differences.

00:17:44.509 --> 00:17:47.790
"Do Orcs uhh have mad hops?

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.039
How many Orcs are ballers?"

00:17:50.039 --> 00:17:51.039
"Excuse me?"

00:17:51.039 --> 00:17:52.039
"You f***in heard the detective.

00:17:52.039 --> 00:17:53.039
How many orcs play pro basketball?"

00:17:53.039 --> 00:17:54.039
"None.

00:17:54.039 --> 00:17:57.070
They're slow, they're heavy, that's why half
the NFL defensive lines are Orcish.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.070
It's not racism.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:17:59.070
It's physics."

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:02.400
And yes, they’re fictional characters who
are literally of different species - but the

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:06.420
metaphor still evokes black Americans and
it feels plain weird.

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:10.759
That’s coupled with Jakoby's clear inability
to match Ward’s intellect, which falls into

00:18:10.759 --> 00:18:15.870
the trap of evoking hundreds of years of junk
racist biology that tried to excuse racism

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:18.990
on the grounds that people of color were intellectually
inferior.

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:23.710
At the same time, giving orcs a super strong
sense of smell gives them an “animalistic”

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:28.670
quality that; Again, makes the metaphor super
ugly, particularly when you consider the hundreds-year

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:32.450
long history of white Americans treating black
American like subhuman beings.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:36.900
As Lakoff and Johnson explains, “What is
at issue is not the truth or falsity of a

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:41.250
metaphor but the perceptions and inferences
that follow from it and the actions that are

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:43.169
sanctioned by it.”

00:18:43.169 --> 00:18:47.830
Metaphors like the one in Bright, in comparing
people of color to inhuman beings, are fucked

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:50.110
up even if they had good intentions.

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:54.630
And as, Lakoff and Johnson explain, “We
define our reality in terms of metaphors and

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.400
then proceed to act.”

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.120
Though it makes some superficial points about
tolerance, and encourages the audience to

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:05.259
empathize with Jakoby’s experiences of marginalization,
abusive friendships and “kick me” signs,

00:19:05.259 --> 00:19:09.070
Bright’s overarching metaphor is just plain
sloppy.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:10.460
Bright let us down.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:15.860
But given our world of inevitable sequels,
threequels and squeakuels, we suspect elves

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:20.559
and orcs will be duking it out on your favorite
streaming platform for years to come.

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:21.700
And we’re not mad at it.

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:26.400
Netflix has a cool concept to work with, and,
in case you haven’t heard, a disgusting

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:28.399
amount of dough to throw at it.

00:19:28.399 --> 00:19:37.750
Here’s hoping the sequel gives us something
special.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:20:20.019
Thanks for watching, guys.

00:20:20.019 --> 00:20:37.529
Peace.

